    "body": "WARSAW, Poland — Czech jet engine producer PBS Aerospace aims to expand its manufacturing capacity in the United States, targeting at least a twofold increase in its production capacity there, according to senior company representatives.\nJan Havránek, PBS Aerospace’s chief government relations and strategy officer, told Defense News that is because the business is booming due to a rising demand for compact propulsion systems used in drones and cruise missiles.\n“Everybody wants to build drones and missiles that are fast, fly far, and they want to have them now. That puts scale and quality at the center of the conversation,” Havránek said. “Global conflicts, including the war in Ukraine, have made clear how important it is for the United States to have a reliable domestic supply of these engines, and the market is moving quickly.”\nWhile continuing to produce engines at its factory in Velká Bíteš, in the Czech Republic, PBS Aerospace expanded its American presence in September 2025 with the launch of a plant in Roswell, Georgia. The manufacturing facility was opened as part of a $20 million investment.\n“We’re building engines in America, for America,” Havránek said. “Our Georgia facility serves the U.S. market first and foremost, with the U.S. government as our end customer. We supply engines to prime contractors that deliver systems to the Department of War, and we’re proud to help strengthen the U.S. defense industrial base and bring critical manufacturing back onshore.”\nIn the U.S., the group’s partners include Lockheed Martin and Pratt & Whitney.\nTo meet rising demand, PBS Aerospace plans to increase its Roswell facility’s annual output to between 10,000 and 20,000 engines over the next three years.\n“For next year, we’re looking at up to 5,000 engines to be produced in America,” he said.\nWith the manufacturing ramp-up in mind, PBS Aerospace is planning to allocate around $100 million to boost its capacity in Roswell, according to Havránek.\nSome of the company’s most sought-after products include the TJ40-G1, a compact jet engine that weighs 3.4 kg (7.5 pounds) and delivers up to 425 N of thrust, as indicated by data from the manufacturer.",
